var e=`# CHAPTER 7 — Innocent

I could've stopped right there and handled it. But I had a moral commitment to Freddy: don't lay a finger on him. And I had a logistics problem, which was Kim on the other side of the room, with no idea the party was over.

So I started walking toward the buffet.

---

Freddy came at me again, winding the punch up from way back with his whole arm. I spun 360 degrees around him, like walking around a lamppost, and kept going. He went straight past and only stopped because he hit a pillar.

There was entourage along the way. One came at me head-on and I dropped him with a jab. Another grabbed me by the shirt and went down with the very chair he tried to pick up. The third one I didn't even really see, I just felt the elbow land.

And behind me, the whole time, Freddy.

— GET BACK HERE!

I slipped left. He missed right. I slipped right. He missed left. It was like a dance, except I was the only one who knew the steps.

---

Meanwhile, at the buffet table.

Kim had a chicken croquette in his mouth, a glass in his left hand and three snacks balanced in his right. He was watching the chaos like somebody watching a bad show on someone else's TV.

Two guys from the entourage stopped in front of him.

— You friends with that green-haired guy?

Kim chewed. Thought. Chewed some more.

— Depends on what he did.

One of them bumped the table and a tray of little meat pies started sliding off the edge.

Kim dropped everything. He caught the tray in midair before it hit the floor, put it back in place, straightened a pie that had fallen out of line, and only then punched the guy in the face.

In that order. Never the other way around.

— Don't touch the table.
`;export{e as default};
